.git
.venv
# Wildcarded (not just literal .env): the Dockerfile's builder stage does
# `COPY . .` into the image, so a same-directory .env.production (real
# secrets, created per DEPLOY.md for docker-compose's env_file: to read at
# RUNTIME) would otherwise get baked into the image layers at BUILD time --
# retrievable by anyone who can pull the image, and not removable by a
# later layer. Neither .env.production.example nor .env.example carries
# secrets, but the backend image needs no .env file at all (docker-compose
# injects real values via env_file: after the image is already built), so
# nothing here needs a negation.
.env*
.claude
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
__pycache__
*.egg-info
build
dist
results
eval_results
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
# The api/worker image (this Dockerfile) needs none of these -- excluding
# them keeps the build context (and therefore every `docker build`) fast.
# web/app has its own Dockerfile + .dockerignore and is built separately
# by docker-compose.yml.
web
graphify-out
.superpowers
.impeccable
docs
tests
node_modules
*.md
# pyproject.toml's readme = "README.md" is read by setuptools during
# `pip install .` -- the blanket *.md exclude above must not eat it.
!README.md

# See .gitignore: private keys must never reach an image layer.
*.key
*.pem
